Subject: keep journal sequence numbers monotonic across mounts

jbd_journal_start() sets the session's first transaction id from the on-disk
journal superblock:

    /*
     * To invalidate any stale records we need to start from
     * the checkpoint transaction ID of the previous journalling session
     * plus 1.
     */
    journal->trans_id = jbd_get32(&jbd_fs->sb, sequence) + 1;

but jbd_journal_stop() then writes that field back as 0:

    journal->start = 0;
    journal->trans_id = 0;
    jbd_journal_write_sb(journal);

so the stored sequence after every clean unmount is 0, the next session always
computes 1, and the stale-record protection the comment describes never
actually happens.

The consequence is a real loss of crash consistency. jbd_journal_start()
publishes s_start pointing at the head of the log before the first transaction
of the session has committed. Crash in that window and the journal superblock
advertises a log that was never written -- but the blocks it points at still
hold records from the *previous* session, carrying the same sequence number 1.
Recovery accepts them and replays stale metadata over the live filesystem.

Observed with a cross-directory rename, cutting the write stream partway
through. Before recovery the volume is intact; the damage is done by replay:

    cut@5: journal sb says s_start=1 s_sequence=1, no commit block written
           -> kernel replays previous session's records
           -> "Unattached zero-length inode 526"
    cut@6: -> "ext4_check_descriptors: Inode bitmap for group 0 not in group"
              (kernel refuses the mount entirely)

Keeping trans_id at stop makes sequence numbers monotonic across mounts, so
records left over from an earlier session can no longer match and recovery
rejects them.

diff --git a/src/ext4_journal.c b/src/ext4_journal.c
index 7874f58..bca3ae5 100644
--- a/src/ext4_journal.c
+++ b/src/ext4_journal.c
@@ -1443,7 +1443,20 @@ int jbd_journal_stop(struct jbd_journal *journal)
 		return r;
 
 	journal->start = 0;
-	journal->trans_id = 0;
+	/*
+	 * Deliberately keep trans_id rather than resetting it to 0.
+	 *
+	 * jbd_journal_start() computes the next session's first transaction id
+	 * as on-disk sequence + 1, specifically so that records still
+	 * physically present in the log from an earlier session cannot be
+	 * mistaken for current ones. Writing 0 here defeats that: every session
+	 * then starts at 1, stale records also carry 1, and a crash that
+	 * advertises a log start before the new transaction commits lets
+	 * recovery replay the previous session's records over live metadata.
+	 *
+	 * Persisting the last id used keeps sequence numbers monotonic across
+	 * mounts, which is what makes the +1 meaningful.
+	 */
 	jbd_journal_write_sb(journal);
 	return jbd_write_sb(journal->jbd_fs);
 }
